Chapter: DhcpIO
  cites Net chapter Dhcp
  cites Net chapter UdpIO
  cites Net chapter NetworkConfig
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The acquisition half of DHCP. `Dhcp.codex` builds the messages and
 parses the replies and says of itself "pure logic -- no I/O", which was
 accurate and was the whole problem: nothing in the tree ever put a
 DISCOVER on the wire, so every program that needed an address declared
 one instead. `cdx-serve` carried `10.0.2.15` and `10.0.2.2` as
 constants, and those are the emulator's NAT subnet, which nothing on a
 real LAN answers to.

 It is the sibling of `UdpIO` and does its I/O through it, so the
 `Network.Read` and `Network.Write` rows are inherited and declared here
 for the same reason.

 THE FOUR-STEP EXCHANGE IS DISCOVER, OFFER, REQUEST, ACK, and the two
 sends are separate act functions rather than one block with a
 conditional in a bind position. A conditional bound with `<-` performs
 its effect and hands back nothing, which compiles clean and reads as a
 failed send.

 THE TRANSACTION ID IS THE CALLER'S. A client that retries must reuse the
 xid of the exchange it is retrying and a client starting fresh must not,
 and this chapter cannot tell which is happening.

 A REFUSAL IS A LEASE THAT SAYS SO. Every failure path answers
 `dhcp-lease-empty`, whose `valid` is False, rather than an address the
 caller might use: a made-up address on a real LAN is a machine that
 transmits into a subnet it does not belong to.

 We say:

Section: The Exchange

 The socket is the DHCP client port talking to the server port at the
 broadcast address. A client has no address yet, so it sources from
 0.0.0.0, which is what `dhcp-build-msg` writes into ciaddr as well.

  dhcp-io-socket : List Integer, List Integer -> UdpSocket
  dhcp-io-socket (mac) (from-ip) =
    udp-io-open mac from-ip dhcp-client-port [255, 255, 255, 255] dhcp-server-port

  dhcp-io-acquire : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] DhcpLease
  dhcp-io-acquire (mac) (xid) (fuel) =
    let sk = dhcp-io-socket mac [0, 0, 0, 0]
    in act
      sent <- udp-io-send sk (dhcp-build-discover xid mac)
      offer <- dhcp-io-await sk fuel dhcp-msg-offer
      dhcp-io-request sk mac xid fuel offer
    end

  dhcp-io-request : UdpSocket, List Integer, Integer, Integer, DhcpLease -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] DhcpLease
  dhcp-io-request (sk) (mac) (xid) (fuel) (offer) =
    if dhcp-is-offer offer == False then dhcp-lease-empty
    else act
      sent <- udp-io-send sk (dhcp-build-request xid mac (offer.client-ip) (offer.server-id))
      dhcp-io-await sk fuel dhcp-msg-ack
    end

Section: Waiting

 The poll is bracketed the way `UdpIO`'s and `NetIO`'s are, and for the
 same reason rather than for symmetry: a miss allocates the datagram it
 rejected, this runs to millions of misses on a quiet wire, and there is
 no collector. The restore does not happen on the hit path, where the
 lease being returned was allocated after the save.

 A DATAGRAM OF THE WRONG TYPE IS A MISS, not a failure. A DHCP client
 hears every other client's broadcast traffic on the same port, so the
 first reply to arrive is not necessarily an answer to this exchange.

  dhcp-io-await : UdpSocket, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read] DhcpLease
  dhcp-io-await (sk) (fuel) (want) =
    if fuel <= 0 then dhcp-lease-empty
    else let hp = __heap-save
    in act
      got <- udp-io-recv sk 1
      if got.ur-got == False then let restored = __heap-restore hp
        in dhcp-io-await sk (fuel - 1) want
      else let lease = dhcp-parse-response (got.ur-data)
      in if lease.valid & lease.msg-type == want then lease
      else let restored2 = __heap-restore hp
      in dhcp-io-await sk (fuel - 1) want
    end

Section: Configuration

 The lease as the rest of the OS wants it. `net-apply-dhcp-ack` is what
 turns a lease into a `NetworkConfig`, and a refused acquisition leaves
 the config in the state it was in rather than half-filled.

  dhcp-io-configure : List Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] NetworkConfig
  dhcp-io-configure (mac) (hostname) (xid) (fuel) (now) =
    let cfg = net-config-empty mac hostname
    in act
      lease <- dhcp-io-acquire mac xid fuel
      dhcp-io-apply cfg lease now
    end

  dhcp-io-apply : NetworkConfig, DhcpLease, Integer -> NetworkConfig
  dhcp-io-apply (cfg) (lease) (now) =
    if dhcp-is-ack lease then net-apply-dhcp-ack cfg lease now
    else cfg

Section: Renewal

 A lease is a loan and half of it is the deadline to ask again. Without
 this a server keeps an address the LAN is entitled to reassign, and the
 symptom arrives long after the boot that caused it: traffic to the old
 address reaches whoever holds it now.

 THE RENEWAL IS NOT AN ACQUISITION. It carries the address the client
 already holds in ciaddr and omits the requested-address and server-id
 options, per RFC 2131 4.3.6; `dhcp-build-renew` is that message.

 IT IS BROADCAST, WHICH IS THE REBINDING FORM RATHER THAN THE RENEWING
 ONE, and that is a deliberate simplification rather than an oversight.
 Renewing unicasts to the server that granted the lease, and this stack
 does not retain which one that was: `NetworkConfig` keeps the gateway
 and the DNS server, and on plenty of networks the DHCP server is
 neither. Broadcasting at T1 is answered by any server holding the
 binding, which is a superset of the one that would answer a unicast,
 and it costs one broadcast frame per half-lease.

 A RENEWAL THAT GETS NO ANSWER KEEPS THE OLD CONFIGURATION rather than
 dropping to unconfigured. The lease is still valid until it expires, the
 caller is still entitled to serve on it, and the next pass asks again --
 a client that discarded a working address because one datagram went
 missing would take itself off the network for the rest of the lease.

 THE FUEL IS THE CALLER'S and it should be small. This runs on a service
 loop between connections, so a renewal that polled for millions of turns
 would stall the thing it exists to keep serving.

  dhcp-io-maybe-renew : NetworkConfig, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] NetworkConfig
  dhcp-io-maybe-renew (cfg) (xid) (fuel) (now) =
    if net-lease-due cfg now == False then cfg
    else dhcp-io-renew cfg xid fuel now

  dhcp-io-renew : NetworkConfig, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] NetworkConfig
  dhcp-io-renew (cfg) (xid) (fuel) (now) =
    let sk = dhcp-io-socket (cfg.local-mac) (cfg.local-ip)
    in act
      sent <- udp-io-send sk (dhcp-build-renew xid (cfg.local-mac) (cfg.local-ip))
      lease <- dhcp-io-await sk fuel dhcp-msg-ack
      dhcp-io-apply cfg lease now
    end
